`Meter` is a mutable C# struct with a public `int Reading` field and an ordinary (not `readonly`) method `public void Tick() { Reading += 5; }`.

```csharp
static void ViaRef(ref Meter m) { m.Tick(); }
static void ViaIn(in Meter m)   { m.Tick(); }

var a = new Meter { Reading = 1 };
var b = new Meter { Reading = 1 };
ViaRef(ref a);
ViaIn(in b);
```

Both methods compile. Afterwards `a.Reading` is `6` but `b.Reading` is still `1`, even though both parameters refer to the caller's variable. Which statement best explains the difference?

In C#, class `Base` has no `Describe` method. Class `Derived : Base` declares an ordinary instance method `public string Describe() => "instance";`. A static class in scope declares the extension method `public static string Describe(this Base b) => "extension";`.

```csharp
Base value = new Derived();
Console.WriteLine(value.Describe());
```

State which `Describe` runs, and explain why that one is chosen.
